DJ Notes:

Song structure is way a song is arranged over time. This includes verse, chorus, bridge.

Verse is usually one of the first section you hear in a song. They typically repeat themselves - not necessarily by lyrics/instrumentation, but most of the time by melodic structure/chords (ex. I Will Survive - Gloria Rayner). *hook

Chorus is usually the catchiest part of the song and is memorable. Typically repeats the same way in lyrics and instrumentation + some energizing percussion. *drop

Bridge serves as a musical breakpoint in a song. It is in the same key and sounds like part of the song. But the key purpose is to retain interest/engagement from the listener by making chorus/other parts of the song stand out. Can be executed with change in lyrics/melody

Common Mistakes To Avoid:
1. Not implementing song structure, ex. Not changing chord/melodic structure to highlight the chorus
2. Changing the sound of each section. You want the song to sound cohesive, albeit retaining song structure (verse, chorus, bridge) to keep the listener engaged/entertained.
3. Changing the length of each section too much. Keep it simple and consistent.

Common Song Structures:
1. Verse - Build - Drop - Verse - Build - Drop (ex. Lean In - Major Lazer)
2. Verse - Chorus - Verse - Chorus - Bridge - Chorus (ex. Demons - Imagine Dragons)
3. Verse - PreChorus - Chorus - Verse - PreChorus - Chorus - Bridge - Chorus (ex. Demons - Imagine Dragons)
4. Chorus - Verse - Chorus - Verse - Chorus - Bridge - Chorus (ex. Low - Flo Rida)
5. Verse -  Chorus - PostChorus - Verse - Chorus - PostChorus - Bridge - Chorus (ex. Ride - Twenty One Pilots)

Phrasing is the way musical notes, rhythm, and lyric are grouped together, similar to sentences and paragraphs in language. If a song is a house, song structure is the blueprint while phrasing is how the furniture is arranged in a room.

Phasing being heard makes sure both tracks are playing on top of each other

Beat is the kick drum you kick your foot to when listening to a song

Bar is a collection of 4 beats

Phrase is a collection of bars - usually 8 to 16 bars

Phrasing mixing is the process of overlapping two tracks together while making sure the musical structure of both tracks are in line and intact

Controller Buttons/Knobs/Sliders Cheat Sheet

Cue (rectangle) - allows to hear song in headphones but not on master output

EQ Mixer Section

Low - controls low frequency band ex. bass
Medium - controls medium range ex. Vocals/melody
High - controls high range
Trim - controls pre-fader volume (fader is the total volume slider at bottom)

Beat FX Section

1, 2, 1&2 slider - controls which decks enable effects
Level/Depth knob - controls how prominent a selected FX is

Common Effects:

Reverb - controls the size of the room a song is played in
Echo
Delay - same as Echo but does not diminish over time

Mixing in Key

When mixing out of key that’s when you hear something is “off”. 
Key Sync - adjusts the pitch of the song by X number of tones to help with mixing in key

Camelot Wheel - musical key is represented by #/letter combo 
￼
T-Method - keys within the T are harmonic to each other
￼

DJ MUSIC SCHOOL NOTES

Chapter 1:
Check your BPM of what you’re playing to checking BPM of your mixing into

Useful tip when beat matching, use the dj platter to adjust speed of track and when it matches adjust the pitch slider to keep the speed persistent

What to look for in a tune? An emotional reaction

Bandcamp a good place to discover music and get your music discovered

Put music in rekordbox through mixed in key for temp and key

Beat is kickdrum (base)
Bar = 4 X Beat = 4 Beats
Downbeat = 4 x Bar = 16 Beats
Section Downbeat = 4 x Downbeat = 64 Beats